 { Weak squares tend to accumulate for chess players along a particular set of colors, or 'color complexes' as they are sometimes referred to. The below position illustrates the chief reason why this is: pawns like to form chains! This arrangement brings many benefits, as each pawn protects its colleague, freeing each side from having to protect their pawns with pieces. The main drawback, however, is that they can leave one side with an imbalance of control along the opposite color. In this case, we can see that Black lacks control of dark squares, while White's weaknesses lie along the light squares. In the section that follows, we'll be dividing the material into three categories: 1. Weak light squares 2. Weak dark squares 3. Weak squares & outposts Let's dive in! } 1. { -KEY- }  
