 { On the queenside, White enjoys strong control over the light squares. However, on the kingside the opposite holds true as all of White's pawns sit on dark squares. Black exploits these weak squares beautifully over the coming moves. } 1... { -KEY- } Qe7 $1 { Threatening both ...Rd8 and ...Re8. } 2. Rd1 { Stopping Black from taking over the d-file. } ( 2. Re1 { also lands White in trouble after } Rd8 $1 3. Rxe7 Rxd3 4. Re3 Rd1 5. Re1 Rxe1 6. Nxe1 Be6 $19 { The bishop picks off the weak pawn on c4. } ) 
Re8 $1 { Shifting the focus to an invasion on e4, when the pawn on c4 will become an easy target. } 3. Qc3 Qb7 $1 { Tying down White's knight. } 4. Rd5 { White's hope is to play 5.Ne5 and be able to perhaps post their knight on c6 in the long run, to completely shut down the diagonal. White is forced to devise artificial-looking plans to patch up the structural weaknesses. } ( 4. Ne1 $2 { It's too late to try to untangle. } Rxe1 $1 5. Rxe1 Qg2 ) 
f6 $1 { Stopping the threat. Black has excellent harmony between their light-squared bishop and their dark-squared pawns. } 5. Nh4 { Trying a different maneuver. If the knight could arrive at the e3 square it would shut down the e-file and cover key light squares. The problem is that it is too time-consuming. } Qe7 $1 { Threatening mate. } 6. Rd1 { By securing the back rank, White renews the threat of Nh4-g2. } ( 6. Ng2 $2 { no longer works since White has no good reply against } Qe4 $1 { For example: } 7. Ne3 Qf3 $19 { White has no good reply against the threat of ...Rxe3. } ) 
Qe2 $1 { Invading and forcing White to spend time moving their rook. } 7. Rc1 Re4 $1 { Pressuring c4 and threatening ...Rd4-d1+. } 8. Ng2 { Finally White brings the knight to g2, but it's too late. } Rd4 $1 9. Qe1 ( 9. Ne3 Rd2 $19 ) 
Qf3 10. Ne3 f5 $1 { The march of Black's f-pawn will decide the game, as once Black plays ...f4 White will be forced to capture the pawn, opening up new lines of attack against their king. } 11. Rd1 f4 $1 12. gxf4 ( 12. Rxd4 fxe3 $19 ) 
Rxf4 $19 { Black will play ...Rg4+ next, when Nxg4 will be met by Qg2#. This line involved complex high-level play, with Black latching on to the light-squared weaknesses in much the same way as the attacks we have seen previously against a specific weak square. It's worth noting how the final breakthrough involved a pawn break. Such moves are frequently useful in conjunction with a color complex strategy as they can soften up the defenses of the opponent. Let's take a look at this in action next. } 
