 { Here it's clear that Black's weaknesses on the kingside lie on the light squares, in particular the f5 and h5 squares. White is therefore interested in playing a move that will increase their relative control over light squares. Can you find the best move here for White? } 1. { -KEY- } Bxf4 $1 { The knight was defending the light squares, so its absence accentuates Black's problems along this color complex. } Bxf4 2. g3 $1 { Gaining time against the bishop and preparing a pawn break. } Bd6 3. h4 $1 { A powerful pawn break! Eventually Black will have to support the g5 pawn with the move ...f6, further weakening the light squares around the king. } Kg7 ( 3... gxh4 { The Black kingside is too weak. Best play would continue: } 4. Qf5 $1 { Threatening to set up a queen-bishop battery with Bf1-d3 next. } Qc8 { Black has no good way to stop this threat, since, for example, this reply runs into a tactical blow: } 5. Re6 $1 { When the point is that: } fxe6 6. Qg6 Kh8 7. Qxh6 Kg8 8. Qg6 Kh8 9. Bd3 Rf5 11. dxe6 { Black's position collapses, since if the rook moves there will be checkmate on h7. } ) 
4. hxg5 hxg5 5. Qf5 $1 { It is here where Black's f-pawn has no choice but to join the dark side! } f6 { Now all of Black's pawns are arranged on dark squares, leaving the opposite color complex chronically weak. To make a bad situation worse, White is the only side with a light-squared bishop, so they will have no difficulty slicing through the last of Black's defenses. } 6. Bd3 $1 { Threatening mate on h7. } Rh8 ( 6... Rg8 { This move would also not have saved Black. For example: } 7. Qh7 Kf8 8. Qh6 Ke8 9. Bg6 Kd7 10. Qh3 { with forced mate. } ) 
7. Qg6+ Kf8 8. Re6 $1 { It's instructive to see how White has posted heavy pieces on both of the advanced weak squares near Black's king. } Rg8 { Black tries to evict the White queen. This is necessary in order for any chance of survival, but here it invites a typical tactic. } 9. Rxf6+ $1 exf6 10. Qxf6+ Ke8 { Black has no choice but to run with their king, but stepping onto a light square proves fatal. } ( 10... Qf7 { Blocking with the queen results in devastating material losses. } 11. Qxd8 Kg7 12. Qxd6 $18 ) 
11. Bb5+ { It is surprisingly common in chess that the weak square that brings the game to a close is often on the other side of the board from where the action has focused. } Rd7 { The only move. } 12. Re1+ Be5 ( 12... Be7 { Blocking in this way is not possible as the d7 rook is pinned. } 13. Qxe7 ) 
13. d6 $1 { An elegant finish. Black cannot take the pawn on d6, since after ...Qxd6 Qxd6 Black cannot capture the queen since both of their pieces are pinned. The main takeaways of White's play here are as follows: (1) Noting what set of squares a knight is defending is useful. White spotted that Black's knight covered the light squares, and exchanged it to increase the chances of success in the attack. (2) Pawn breaks can be powerful as they may force other pawns to move forward to defend the weakened structure. In the game, we saw that the h2-h4 pawn break eventually forced Black into playing ...f6, further weakening the light squares. (3) An attack along a set of colors is greatly enhanced if the attacking side possesses the bishop of the same color as the opponent's weaknesses. White's light-squared bishop was instrumental in the attack. The next two examples will build on our knowledge as we now turn to typical themes relating to weakened dark squares. } 
