 { Can you find the best move here for White to begin an attack against Black's weak squares? } 3. { -KEY- } Qd4 $1 { White steps the queen to a dark square, gaining time against Black's undefended rook. } f6 { Black moves the pawn to a dark square, not only to defend the rook but to try to patch up the dark-squared weaknesses on the kingside. } ( 3... Re8 $2 { makes it even easier for White after } 4. Ng4 $1 { Threatening mate in 2: Nh6+ followed by Qh8#. } f6 { Black has nothing better than to give back the pawn in order to stay alive. But after: } 5. Nxf6 Nxf6 6. Qxf6 $18 { White is once again up a pawn, but this time with Black's king safety a permanent concern. } ) 
4. f4 { Gaining time and preparing a pawn break with f4-f5. } Re8 5. f5 $1 { The drawback of the previous move ...f6 for Black is that they weakened their g6 pawn. With this move, White switches their focus onto the light squares. } g5 6. Qg4 $1 Ng7 { The more active ...Nf4 offered greater resistance, but even there White was already winning with best play. } 7. h4 $1 h6 { Much like in the previous example, White's pawn break forces Black to put another pawn on a dark square. In an amusing turn of events, Black has gone from having terribly weak dark squares to having terribly weak light squares! As the attacker, it's important to be flexible and be willing to transform one advantage into another when the opportunity arises. } ( 7... gxh4 8. Qxh4 $18 { Black has no defense against Ne3-g4. } ) 
8. hxg5 hxg5 9. Bc2 $18 { Finally White's pieces are ready to invade along the light squares. } Qd7 10. Bb3+ Kf8 11. Qh3 $18 { Black has no defense against Qh8+. } 
