 { In the previous game, we saw how White had a knight on the e3 square against a kingside that lacked the defense of the dark-squared bishop. In this case, the same holds true. A notable difference here is that there are three knights on the board. This brings to mind the question, asked in jest, to Magnus Carlsen at the 2021 World Championship by content creator Andrea Botez. While her question, 'how does the knight move?' was done in good humor and as a way of creating content, it turns out that this question is surprisingly important in the context of targeting a color complex. A knight will always control the opposite color of where it is stationed, and it's important to keep this in mind when judging the likelihood of your attack succeeding. To show what we mean by this, let's consider the following position from the online game Magnus Carlsen - Nikolas Theodorou (2022). } 1. { -KEY- } fxg6 { White begins by creating a semi-open file. } ( 1. Ng4 { The immediate jump was equally strong. } ) 
hxg6 { Black recaptures with the h-pawn to keep their structure as solid as possible, but the position is already losing. } 2. Ng4 $1 { Taking a snapshot of the knight positioning, we can see that White is in excellent shape: 1. Their knight eyes up the weak f6 and h6 squares. 2. The knight on g7 is not guarding the dark squares. 3. The knight on d7 is guarding f6, but not h6. Also, the knight is vulnerable to being captured if necessary, especially if White can play a move like Qd2-g5 or Qd2-h6. } f5 { Black had to respond to the threat of Nh6+ followed by capturing on f7. } ( 2... Nf5 $6 { From here the knight can cover the dark squares, but White can eliminate it! } 3. Rxf5 $1 gxf5 4. Qg5 Kf8 5. Nh6 $18 { With either 6. Qg8+ or 6. Nxf5 both leading to a quick checkmate. } ) 
3. Nh6+ Kh7 ( 3... Kf8 { Running with the king to f8 is also no good, since after } 4. Qd6 Re7 5. Qxg6 $18 { Black's defenses collapse. } ) 
4. Nf7 $1 { The attacked rook on d8 is the least of Black's problems! White is threatening to invade on h6. } Ne6 ( 4... f4 { Black can try to shut down access to h6, but this move weakens the light squares and White can use this fact to collect enormous amounts of material. } 5. Nxd8 Rxd8 6. Bg4 $18 { Black cannot move the knight without losing the rook on d8. } ) 
5. Qh6+ $1 { The invasion of the queen leads to a quick finish. } Kg8 6. Qxg6+ Ng7 7. c5 { Black resigned in view of the threat of Be2-c4 next. For your own games, I would advise paying special attention to the placement, as well as the potential, of your knights. They are tricky pieces, and it is much harder to estimate their effect on color complexes than with any other piece due to their unique movement on the chess board. Next, let's discuss the important link between color complexes and outposts. } 
