 { Earlier in the chapter, we showed a game where Magnus Carlsen beat GM Alexander Rustemov by targeting the weak d6 square. In this example, we see the same two players, the same outcome, and even the same critically important d6 square in play! This example illustrates the important role that outposts can play in the battle for dominating a color complex. } 8... { -KEY- } Bb4+ { Black's opening play has been quite unconventional, prioritizing kingside development at the expense of weaknesses along the dark squares. Now GM Rustemov offers a bishop trade, a risky strategy since this piece is the main defender of the dark squares. } 9. Bd2 Qa5 $6 { Black defends their bishop and may have hoped that the piece tension would be problematic for White. } 10. c5 $1 { Magnus takes the opportunity to 'clamp down' on the dark squares. The weak b6 and d6 squares at the start of the position have now been converted into outposts. } Nd7 11. a3 { Forcing the immediate exchange and also preparing an eventual b2-b4 advance. } Bxd2+ 12. Nxd2 { The knight will be able to maneuver to c4 soon, from where it will eye up the b6 and d6 outposts. } O-O 13. b4 ( 13. O-O-O $5 { This bold move may have been even stronger, but Magnus likely felt that the safer approach was to eventually castle kingside. } ) 
Qc7 $2 ( 13... Qd8 $1 { ∞ Black needed to drop the queen back to d8 and seek counterplay quickly with either ...a5 or ...e5. Not only is he being targeted on the dark squares, but he is being squeezed for space. We'll take a look at the topic of space in detail a couple of chapters from now. } ) 
14. Bd3 { Developing, with a threat of checkmate. } g6 ( 14... Nf6 { With the queen on c7 versus d8, this way of defending loses its bite as now White can gain time against her. } 15. Qe5 $1 Qxe5 { The computer indicates that Black has nothing better than to exchange queens, but this is a depressing middlegame to play. } 16. dxe5 Nd7 17. Nc4 $16 { Black's total loss of control over the dark squares has a devastating effect on his minor pieces, with neither the knight nor the bishop having any prospects for activity. The position is already close to winning for White. } ) 
 { Given the problem of playing 14...Nf6, GM Rustemov blocks the threat of mate on h7 with a pawn. However, with this he moves his last remaining dark-squared pawn onto a light square, worsening his positional problems. } 15. Nc4 $1 { In addition to eyeing up the central outpost on d6, the White knight is also overprotecting the e5 square, making the liberating pawn break impossible. Meanwhile, Black's light-squared bishop remains bricked in on its starting square. } e5 { In desperation, Black tries the pawn break anyway. } 16. Nd6 $1 { Magnus correctly judges that the positional approach is the best way to starve Black of practical chances, rather than risk complications after dxe5. } exd4 17. O-O $1 { Stepping off the e-file. The d4 pawn is not going anywhere. } Nf6 18. Qxd4 $16 ( { Here Black had had enough and resigned. The computer suggests } 18. Qxd4 Ne8 { as the only path to keep the game alive, but the position continues to look grim after a natural response from White such as } 19. Bc4 { or } 19. Rad1 { .Let's take a look at one more example where outposts played a major role in the attack along a color complex. } ) 

