 { This position is taken from the game Yemelin,V - Shulman,Y (1994). The opening has not gone well for Black. White has more space and better central control. They have many different paths that promise a big advantage, but they go for an approach that is aimed at securing outposts along Black's vulnerable dark squares. } 14. { -KEY- } c5 $1 { Unless Black can break this bind, their queenside development will be paralysed. } Ng4 { Black may have wanted to win White's dark-squared bishop in order to take a bit of the sting away from White's strategy of dominating the dark squares. } ( 14... d6 $2 { Exchanging pawns also loses material, after: } 15. cxd6 Bxd6 { 15...Qxd6 16. e5 leads to the same outcome. } 16. e5 $18 { White wins a piece. } ) 
 ( 14... Bxc5 $2 { Capturing the pawn loses material after } 15. Rac1 b6 16. b4 $18 { winning a piece. } ) 
15. Nb6 $1 { The first of White's minor pieces invade the Black camp, ignoring the attack on the bishop. } Rb8 { For as long as White's queenside bind along the dark squares remains in place, the Black rook will remain paralysed. } 16. Nb3 $1 { White's focus is entirely on maintaining dark-squared control. This move also comes with the threat of 17.a3, winning the bishop. } a5 { Black wants to play ...a4, in order to drive the knight away and secure the a5 square as a retreating square for his bishop. } ( 16... d6 { This move doesn't work tactically. For example: } 17. cxd6 Qxd6 18. Rac1 O-O 19. a3 $18 { Black's bishop is trapped. } ) 
17. a3 $1 { Forcing the gain of Black's important dark-squared bishop. } a4 18. axb4 axb3 { The initial defect in Black's position (the weak dark squares) remains on the board, but now White has also added an open a-file and the weak b3 pawn to his list of plusses. } 19. Rfd1 { A typical mistake of less experienced players would be to rush to grab the b3 pawn. White understands that this pawn is doomed and so focuses on adding piece control to the d6 outpost. } O-O 20. Rd6 $18 { It's instructive to note about how, in our starting position, White's move c4-c5 fixed outposts on b6 and d6. Now, just six moves later, two of White's pieces are using these outposts to lodge themselves in the heart of the Black position. In doing so, Black's queenside pieces have been eliminated from play. White is already winning, but we'll show the game until the end, as GM Yemelin's play continues to be highly instructive. } Nf6 { Black realises that capturing the dark-squared bishop is of no concern to White, so instead he seeks to transfer his knight to e8 to try to break the blockade. } 21. Bd4 $1 { White proceeds as if the threat does not exist! } Ne8 22. e5 $1 { The point! White does not mind losing the exchange, since so many of Black's pieces are permanently out of the game. } g6 { Black tries to save their h-pawn, but now their kingside dark squares become even weaker. } ( 22... Nxd6 { Taking the rook leads to a hopeless bind. } 23. exd6 Qd8 ( 23... Qc6 $2 { is even worse, when after } 24. Qd3 $1 { Black's queen is trapped and will be lost after }  25. b5 $1 ) 
24. Qxh5 $18 { One of White's many possibilities here is to play Re1-e3 and then play either Rg3 or Rh3 with a crushing attack. White's rook is fully mobile as it is not needed to control the a-file since White's knight controls the entry point on a8. } ) 
23. Bf2 $1 { A multi-purpose move, opening up possibilities like 24.Qxb3, 24.Qd3 and 24.Bh4 on the next move. } ( 23. f5 $5 { The pawn breakthrough was already a strong option, but White likely wanted to keep risk to a minimum. } ) 
f6 { Black is desperate to open up lines for his pieces, but now the g6 pawn becomes weak. } 24. Qd3 $1 Kf7 25. Bh4 Nxd6 { Finally, Black cannot stand the rook's presence any longer, so he decides to capture it. } 26. Qxd6 $1 { A clever practical decision! Many players would be tempted to keep the queens on the board since, after all, White is the one with the greater attacking chances. The benefit of forcing the queens off the board, however, is that White keeps any risk down to an absolute minimum. } ( 26. exd6 { was also winning, but with the queens on the board there is a higher chance that a mistake might creep into White's play. } ) 
Qxd6 ( 26... Qd8 { was not possible due to } 27. Qxb8 { . } ) 
27. cxd6 $1 { The c-file opens up for a future invasion of White's rook onto the 7th rank. } f5 ( 27... fxe5 { Opening up the f-file will only favour White, as Black will not be in time to use it. } 28. fxe5 Ke8 29. Bf6 $18 { All of Black's pieces remain paralysed, with Rc1 coming next. } ) 
28. Ra3 { Finally, it is time to collect the pawn. } Re8 29. Rxb3 Kg7 30. Bf6+ Kf7 31. Kf2 $1 { The structure of the position reminds us of our introductory color complex position, with White's pawns on the dark squares while Black's pawns are chained along light. Since Black is completely paralysed, White proceeds to improve his king, zig-zagging along the weak dark squares to take up position in enemy territory. } Rf8 32. Kg3 Re8 33. Kh4 Kg8 34. Kg5 { The king has arrived, placing the Black pawn on g6 under fire. } Kh7 35. Rc3 { Finally, having collected the b3 pawn and improved his king as much as possible, White sets his aims at the invasion on the 7th rank. } Rf8 36. Rc7 Re8 37. Nxd7 { The final break-through. The combination of rook, bishop and king will deliver checkmate in a handful of moves, and so Black resigned. I hope you enjoyed this game as much as I did. To me, it is a beautiful demonstration of domination along a particular set of colors. It is also very instructive to see GM Yemelin's patience in converting his advantage, keeping risk minimal whenever possible. Outposts will be discussed in depth in the next chapter, but for now it is worth remembering that weakness of a particular color complex will tend to give rise to advanced outposts which can be used to devastating effect. Next we'll move onto our final example, where we will see how Magnus Carlsen pieces together several aspects of color complex strategy to win a nice game against GM Vladimir Fedoseev. This will be our final game of the section and indeed of the entire chapter, so soon it will be time for a well-deserved break! } 
