 { In this final game of the chapter, we will study how Magnus Carlsen weaves together various techniques and elements of the position in order to break down Black's defenses along the dark squares. No mean feat, as his opponent in the game was the very strong Grandmaster from Russia, Vladimir Fedoseev. Let's begin by listing some initial observations of our starting position: 1. Black's weaknesses on the kingside are mainly along the dark squares. 2. Black's knight on b5 is on a light square, and therefore it controls dark squares. 3. White possesses the unopposed dark-squared bishop. 4. Black is potentially vulnerable along the back rank. Let's dive into the game and show how Magnus combines these elements to conduct his attack against the Black king. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxb5 $1 { The first step and the only path to an advantage. White eliminates Black's knight, thus gaining control over the central d4 square. } Rxb5 2. d6 $1 { In the second step, White breaks in the centre. The goal is to eliminate Black's only dark-squared pawn on that part of the board. } ( 2. Qd4 $2 Nf6 $15 { Black's control of the f6 square would have allowed them to turn the tables. Now the pawn on d5 is lost, and if anyone stands better, it's Black. } ) 
exd6 { Not the toughest defense, but Black was in trouble no matter what he did. } ( 2... e6 { Ignoring the pawn would not be enough to survive the position. Let's examine the variations here as they contain many beautiful tactical ideas. } 3. Qd4 $1 { Threatening mate on g7. } Qf6 ( 3... Nf6 { Black can also try to block the queen's path, but without Black's pawn on e7 White can now use tactics to pry away the defender of the knight. } 4. Rc8 $1 { A beautiful deflecting sacrifice. } Qxc8 5. Qxf6 Qc3 { Black uses the c3 outpost to hang on, but White's next move will collapse his position. } 6. d7 $3 { A beautiful tactic, ignoring the threat against both of White's remaining heavy pieces. } Rbb8 { The rook steps back to defend, but in doing so it loses control over the e5 square. } ( 6... Qxe1 7. Kh2 $18 { Black cannot stop both mating threats. } ) 
7. Re5 $1 { Blocking the contact between the queens, and renewing the threat of Qg7#. } Qxe5 8. Qxe5 $18 { White will mate on g7 in the next few moves. } ) 
4. Qa7 $1 { A sudden shift to the queenside leaves Black without a good answer to the attack against his knight. } Rd8 { Black has to defend the knight in order to stop the d-pawn from advancing. } 5. f4 $1 { Strengthening the grip over key dark squares. Black has no good way to stop the threat of 6.Bg5. For example: } Qf5 $2 6. Qxd7 $1 { Capitalising on Black's loss of control over the back rank. } Rxd7 7. Rc8 Rd8 8. Rxd8 { It is unsurprising that White came out on top in the complications, since tactical opportunities tend to show up in favor of the side with the better position. } ) 
3. Rxe8+ $1 { The third step. The exchange of rooks further impacts Black's ability to defend the dark squares. } Qxe8 { With the Black queen forced off the h4-d8 diagonal, her ability to defend the dark squares is limited. } 4. Qd4 $1 { Black's weakened dark squares and poor piece coordination leaves him with no good way of stopping the threat of mate on g7. } Ne5 { Black has to shut down the diagonal in some way, but now White wins a piece. } ( 4... f6 $2 5. Qc4 { picks up the rook. } ) 
 ( 4... Qe5 $2 5. Rc8 { with checkmate on the next move. } ) 
5. f4 { From here Magnus guides the game into a winning endgame: } Rc5 6. Rxc5 dxc5 7. Qxe5 $18 Qxe5 { Black's queenside pawns look threatening, but White's bishop will soon stop their advance. } 8. fxe5 c4 9. Kf2 b3 10. axb3 cxb3 11. Bc1 { The pawns have been stopped, and White's king will march to the queenside to collect them. The game continued for a few more moves before Black resigned. I hope this game proved helpful to recap previous concepts as well as to show how you can tie together various elements in a position to exploit your opponent's weak color complex. A quick and perhaps scary fact about this game. It wasn't a classical, or even a rapid game...it was a 3-minute blitz game! I don't know if to be inspired or terrified by Carlsen's ability to play this complex middlegame to perfection with probably no more than two minutes left on his clock by the time he reached our starting position. To my eyes, this feels superhuman. As a follow-up to this point, I spent a long time analysing many of Carlsen's blitz and rapid games in preparation for this course, and found the process to be very instructive. On some level, more so than his classical games, as the shorter time control makes the ideas simpler and more accessible. For anyone who is looking for new study methods, it might be worth checking if this approach appeals to you. We have now covered all the major topics relating to weak squares. Next, we will quickly review some of the main takeaways of this section, before you get a chance to put the lessons of this chapter into practice with a selection of puzzles. } 
 *
